{"questions":[{"question_id":"D2kSpusVKhN8Jqi8","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2008","year":2008,"examDate":2008,"subject":"physics","chapter":"units-and-measurements","chapterGroup":"mechanics","topic":"screw-gauge","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"two-full-turns-of-the-circular-scale-of-a-screw-gauge-cover-2008-marks-4-d2kspusvkhn8jqi8.htm","content":"Two full turns of the circular scale of a screw gauge cover a distance of 1 mm on its main scale. The\ntotal number of divisions on the circular scale is 50. Further, it is found that the screw gauge has a\nzero error of − 0.03 mm while measuring the diameter of a thin wire, a student notes the main scale\nreading of 3 mm and the number of circular scale divisions in line with the main scale as 35. The\ndiameter of the wire is","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"3.32 mm"},{"identifier":"B","content":"3.73 mm"},{"identifier":"C","content":"3.67 mm"},{"identifier":"D","content":"3.38 mm"}],"correct_options":["D"],"explanation":"Least count of screw gauge = <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.816ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.013ex\" height=\"2.773ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -864.9 3099.7 1225.5\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(318.3,-345) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-n